The first beta of iOS 27.2 includes Apple's redesigned Health app, giving developers a chance to try out the features ahead of launch later this year.<br/>
<br/>
<img class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1063937" src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/apple-ios-27-health-app-watch.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1125" /><br/>
Apple's developer notes for the feature say the Health app is being tested in this beta, but it won't be in the launch version of iOS 27.2. It will instead come later in the year.<br/>
<br/>
The Health app will incorporate <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-intelligence/">Apple Intelligence</a> to turn data and metrics collected by the Apple Watch into personalized guidance. Here are the new features we can expect:<br/>
<ul><br/>
 	<li><strong>Insights</strong> - The Insights tab offers an Apple Intelligence-generated summary of timely information like your latest sleep metrics or a new peak in VO2 max. There are options to get custom advice on readiness, fitness, heart health, and more in the For You section.</li><br/>
 	<li><strong>Personal Guidance</strong> - Personal guidance is part of the Insights tab. It knows what's important to you, and will make relevant suggestions. Apple says if you're working on your cardio, you might see a suggestion to incorporate intervals into your morning run. Recommendations adapt to each user and their data.</li><br/>
 	<li><strong>Readiness</strong> - The new Apple Watch models calculate a readiness score that takes into account recent activity, vitals, and sleep score. It gives a 0–10 score with recommendations on activity level. Readouts include Recover, Pace Yourself, Ready, or Go For It. Info is shown on the watch, in the Fitness app, and in the Health app's Insights tab.</li><br/>
 	<li><strong>Fitness</strong> - Insights incorporates your daily activity and it updates throughout the day.</li><br/>
 	<li><strong>Vitals</strong> - The Vitals option in the Health app has new overnight views and daytime views. HRV is incorporated as a metric, offering a better look at your ability to recover from stress. It measures overall HRV and recovery HRV.</li><br/>
 	<li><strong>Longevity</strong> - There is a new Longevity tab in the Health app. It uses metrics from your iPhone and Apple Watch to assess overall health across heart health, sleep, mental wellbeing, movement health, metabolic health, hearing, and nutrition. The section shows you how well you're doing in each category, and how each metric is classified against the latest clinical guidance.</li><br/>
 	<li><strong>Health Age</strong> - Health Age is part of the Longevity tab. It uses metrics like VO2 max, resting heart rate, HRV, sleep, and biomarkers from blood work to determine how your health metrics compare to your actual age. It will help you figure out what habits make the biggest impact on your health, and how you can improve.</li><br/>
</ul><br/>
At-home health assessments like a VO2 max test will be coming to the Health app, taking advantage of the iPhone's camera to track your movements as you follow instructions from a trainer. There will be in-depth physical assessments for measuring flexibility, strength, balance, and movement mechanics in addition to VO2 max.<br/>
<br/>
Apple also plans to eventually offer U.S. users the option to buy a bloodwork panel from Quest that covers more than 50 biomarkers. The test is priced at &#36;119, and users will be able to order it right in the Health app.<br/>
<br/>
Not all of the new features are going to be available in the beta. <a href="https://www.cnet.com/health/apple-health-app-hits-developer-beta-inside-scoop-apple-vp-health/"><em>CNET</em> says</a> ordering labs, personalized health info in the Insights tab, and personalized recommendations will not be available until later in the year. Features are going to roll out progressively. <br/>
